    "summary": "Acoustic gems, fiery second set and soaring third‑set jams",
    "blurb": "The first set delivers a tight acoustic groove highlighted by Iko Iko and Dark Hollow. The electric second set burns through Loser, Deal and a rapturous Little Red Rooster, while the third set strings together He's Gone → The Wheel → Stella Blue and an inspired Estimated Prophet. Overall the night is high‑energy with standout solos from Jerry and solid support throughout.",
    "review": "On October 7 1980 the Dead rolled into the Warfield for a three‑set marathon that has become a touchstone of their early‑80s era. The acoustic opener – Iko Iko, Dark Hollow, Rosalie McFall and a plaintive El Paso segue into Ripple – set a warm, intimate tone that many fans still cite as a “high water mark.”  The second set flips the switch: Loser erupts into a tight groove, Deal rockets forward, and Jerry’s lead guitar goes “out of control,” especially on Little Red Rooster where his slide work is described as glue‑like.  Phil provides a steady bass backbone, punctuated by a memorable burst during Let It Grow.  Brent’s keyboards shine on Estimated Prophet, adding that signature ’80s texture while Mickey stays out of Space and returns with a light “Mickey Mouse” touch that still drives the rhythm.  The third set is a tour‑de‑force – He’s Gone → The Wheel → Stella Blue flows seamlessly into I Need A Miracle and Good Lovin’, capped by an Alabama Getaway encore.  Across all sources the show is praised for its energy, seamless segues, and Jerry’s soaring solos, though a few recordings note occasional mud in the third set and a brief digital hiccup on Don’t Ease Me In.\n\nFor listeners seeking the clearest picture of this night, the Owsley‑mixed soundboard capture (gd1980-10-07.108882.sbd‑stanley.blackwood‑waddell.flac16) offers excellent fidelity and a pristine mix that lets every nuance shine.",
